00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.390 align:start position:0%
Ubisoft upcoming take on the strategy  genre is a somewhat unique one called  Champions of interior the game has both  elements of turn-based city-building and  real-time MOBA combat with fights played  out by controlling three unique heroes  at once the game is medieval fantasy  themes with the usual swords magic and  castles setting the scene but anterior'  doesn't take itself too seriously the  narrator of the story is quick to make  light-hearted jokes whenever you can and  they set the game's tone even if they  are a little bit lame combat is the main  focus in interior the game's beginning  place me in control of three heroes in  an adventure gameplay mode which puts  you in an objective based fight on a  top-down map objectives go between claim  X number of outposts kill X number of  enemies and defeat boss I could move the  heroes as a group and micromanage each  of their individual skills the main  feature of anteriors combat system is  the elemental wheel which is five  elements that interact in a sort of rock  scissors paper type relationship so  water beats fire fire beats metal metal  beats nature nature beats lightning and  lightning beats water everything else in  between just as normal damage for  example metal element moves inflict 100  percent of damage to water each hero has  skills that can be cast using the qw e  or R keys and you choose which three  heroes you want to bring in to battle  with you when you're controlling all  three heroes at once coordinating  attacks requires a bit of  micromanagement but skills aren't  particularly different between heroes  thankfully and Syria lets you pause at  any time during battle and it will also  automatically pause as soon as you  triggered any fight to let you know that  you're entering combat I found that the  game's unit Pathfinder would often be a  source of frustration heroes would run  around or glitch in their movement in  strange and baffling ways like running  around in longer ways than necessary or  running into each other more than once I  had to manually redirect a units  movement to stop it riding around like a  headless chicken it's not necessary to  fight every enemy on the map and there  are times when avoiding enemies was  crucial this is because heroes didn't  have any natural health regen and you're  forced to rely on bringing potions in to  heal this is where the resource  management side of champions of  anterior' comes in and where the game  becomes turn  essentially you're given a village to  manage and defend building a village  isn't as freeform as say traditional RTS  games with anterior and said restricting  building placements to select sectors of  land certain sectors give benefits to  specific buildings that are placed on  them such as rivers granting 50% more  production to water and lightening  material more importantly building serve  as a way to get resources that assist  with your battle because I had to rely  on potions to keep my team alive during  missions I had to restock them quite  regularly so when I was managing my  village I placed a building that would  give me a water carrier who would help  keep my water supplies topped up and  also placed an alchemy building that  allowed me to brew more consumable  potions to take with me into battle  since the village screen is turn-based  the world only progressed once I clicked  the button to finish my turn this  allowed for me to take time to make  decisions regarding my village base my  base was made up of plots of land that  each contained a specific number of  sectors and when I had filled those up  with buildings I needed to claim a new  plot of land in order to expand  expanding is done in the world map which  presented me with branching options for  me to conquer Ubisoft boasts about  anteriors nonlinear path to victory with  multiple options - the end goal marked  out on this screen each plot of land was  marked out with the benefits they  carried with rewards ranging from the  allegiance of a new hero to more gold in  my time playing the beta I was reminded  a bit of Warcraft but Warcraft was  powered by a rich and deeper law heavy  story with characters who I grew to love  and actually cared about something that  anterior doesn't quite have which means  the interior has to rely on its  mechanics which aren't quite unique  enough to get excited about and not  enough to carry the game on their own  having turn-based city management was a  nice twist but I'm curious if the full  game will offer something a little more  to draw me into a fantasy world that  otherwise feels a bit tired and flat
 

